WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:02.848
A passage talks about your job interview.

00:00:03.024 --> 00:00:05.529
It repeats important words, sounds

00:00:05.549 --> 00:00:07.978
related, and sits near your question in a

00:00:07.998 --> 00:00:10.310
vector space. Is that enough to make it

00:00:10.330 --> 00:00:11.457
the result you need?

00:00:11.645 --> 00:00:13.686
Predicting language and retrieving a

00:00:13.706 --> 00:00:15.929
useful passage reward different

00:00:15.949 --> 00:00:17.075
relationships.

00:00:19.481 --> 00:00:21.467
Pooling a pretrained model gives a

00:00:21.487 --> 00:00:23.116
complete-text vector.

00:00:23.291 --> 00:00:25.423
It does not automatically arrange

00:00:25.443 --> 00:00:27.859
independently encoded questions near

00:00:27.879 --> 00:00:29.289
relevant passages.

00:00:29.438 --> 00:00:31.274
Retrieval-oriented training supplies

00:00:31.294 --> 00:00:33.787
examples of the comparisons the system

00:00:33.807 --> 00:00:35.307
should learn to prefer.

00:00:36.190 --> 00:00:39.177
The chapter asks: Am I being delulu about

00:00:39.197 --> 00:00:40.259
getting this job?

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:43.210
For this teaching case, interpret the need

00:00:43.231 --> 00:00:45.621
as help me judge whether my expectations

00:00:45.641 --> 00:00:46.999
are unrealistic.

00:00:47.187 --> 00:00:49.048
That choice determines the labels we are

00:00:49.068 --> 00:00:50.259
about to assign.

00:00:50.833 --> 00:00:53.197
The labelled positive is How to recognise

00:00:53.217 --> 00:00:55.633
unrealistic expectations during a job

00:00:55.653 --> 00:00:58.455
search. It addresses that stated need

00:00:58.475 --> 00:01:00.272
without repeating the slang.

00:01:00.434 --> 00:01:02.411
The example makes a desired ranking

00:01:02.431 --> 00:01:04.937
explicit; it does not demonstrate that a

00:01:04.957 --> 00:01:07.282
pretrained model already understands

00:01:07.302 --> 00:01:08.248
delulu.

00:01:09.168 --> 00:01:11.626
One competing passage is A history of

00:01:11.646 --> 00:01:13.134
agricultural employment.

00:01:13.296 --> 00:01:15.853
It concerns work, but offers little help

00:01:15.873 --> 00:01:17.825
with the stipulated question about

00:01:17.845 --> 00:01:19.989
unrealistic expectations.

00:01:20.165 --> 00:01:22.619
The training example labels it an easy

00:01:22.639 --> 00:01:23.598
negative.

00:01:24.164 --> 00:01:27.108
An easy contrast can help define the task,

00:01:27.141 --> 00:01:28.822
but it need not teach the finer

00:01:28.842 --> 00:01:30.665
distinctions among closely related

00:01:30.685 --> 00:01:33.423
results. Choosing the negative examples

00:01:33.443 --> 00:01:34.814
changes the lesson.

00:01:34.951 --> 00:01:37.675
A system can separate unrelated topics

00:01:37.695 --> 00:01:40.382
while still confusing different needs

00:01:40.402 --> 00:01:42.340
inside the same topic.

00:01:43.261 --> 00:01:45.344
The hard negative is Ten signs that you

00:01:45.364 --> 00:01:47.586
performed well in your job interview.

00:01:47.735 --> 00:01:49.958
It shares the broad topic and important

00:01:49.978 --> 00:01:53.128
vocabulary. Under our stated need, it does

00:01:53.148 --> 00:01:54.919
not address whether the searcher's

00:01:54.939 --> 00:01:57.289
expectations are unrealistic.

00:01:57.830 --> 00:02:00.202
Change the need to estimating your chances

00:02:00.222 --> 00:02:02.664
from how the interview went, and this

00:02:02.684 --> 00:02:04.467
passage might be useful.

00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:06.697
Positive and negative labels record a

00:02:06.717 --> 00:02:08.939
task's intended distinction.

00:02:09.101 --> 00:02:11.375
They do not reveal an eternal relevance

00:02:11.395 --> 00:02:13.836
label attached to the passage.

00:02:14.746 --> 00:02:16.775
Training updates model parameters to

00:02:16.795 --> 00:02:18.927
favour the labelled positive over its

00:02:18.947 --> 00:02:21.904
competitors. Triplet loss and contrastive

00:02:21.924 --> 00:02:24.507
objectives implement this idea in

00:02:24.527 --> 00:02:25.667
different ways.

00:02:25.816 --> 00:02:27.935
The chapter does not claim a particular

00:02:27.955 --> 00:02:29.932
score for these passages.

00:02:30.492 --> 00:02:33.242
Our invented two-dimensional example uses

00:02:33.262 --> 00:02:36.412
unit-length vectors and cosine similarity.

00:02:36.574 --> 00:02:39.338
Initially, the hard negative scores above

00:02:39.358 --> 00:02:41.825
the positive. In the desired arrangement,

00:02:41.845 --> 00:02:43.989
the positive scores highest.

00:02:44.152 --> 00:02:46.824
The two pictures illustrate an ordering,

00:02:47.038 --> 00:02:49.583
not a measured training run, a universal

00:02:49.603 --> 00:02:52.610
trajectory, or probabilities of relevance.

00:02:53.511 --> 00:02:56.075
Sentence BERT adapted BERT so that

00:02:56.095 --> 00:02:58.536
independently encoded sentences could be

00:02:58.556 --> 00:02:59.979
compared efficiently.

00:03:00.154 --> 00:03:02.415
Dense Passage Retrieval applied the

00:03:02.435 --> 00:03:05.160
bi-encoder approach to questions and

00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:08.123
passages, training relevant passages to

00:03:08.143 --> 00:03:09.785
score above competitors.

00:03:10.334 --> 00:03:12.490
Both illustrate the transition from a

00:03:12.510 --> 00:03:14.423
general language model to useful

00:03:14.443 --> 00:03:16.897
comparisons for an intended task.

00:03:17.072 --> 00:03:19.243
The family name alone does not make the

00:03:19.263 --> 00:03:21.781
transition. Pooling, the training

00:03:21.801 --> 00:03:24.861
examples, the negatives, and the objective

00:03:24.881 --> 00:03:26.871
all help determine the resulting

00:03:26.891 --> 00:03:27.992
representation.

00:03:28.901 --> 00:03:31.106
The space reflects its starting model,

00:03:31.243 --> 00:03:34.444
training pairs, negative examples, loss

00:03:34.464 --> 00:03:36.583
function and optimisation.

00:03:36.758 --> 00:03:39.522
Those ingredients influence which query

00:03:39.542 --> 00:03:41.815
and document relationships receive high

00:03:41.835 --> 00:03:44.986
scores. Better performance on one task

00:03:45.006 --> 00:03:47.589
does not establish usefulness for every

00:03:47.609 --> 00:03:48.426
search.

00:03:48.990 --> 00:03:51.682
Ask why a passage received its label and

00:03:51.702 --> 00:03:53.615
whether the negatives test distinctions

00:03:53.635 --> 00:03:54.916
that matter to you.

00:03:55.078 --> 00:03:57.236
Then evaluate the retriever against the

00:03:57.256 --> 00:03:59.994
intended task. The next film follows the

00:04:00.014 --> 00:04:02.880
labels back to their sources: clicks,

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:05.780
answers, citations and other proxies for a

00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:06.836
useful result.
